			"widgets_values":[
				"Global Metadata: A Mandopop / C-pop guofeng ballad in the East Asian ballad & heritage pop family, with Chinese traditional instruments over modern cinematic-pop production; slow, breathing four-four around 68-76 BPM, gentle half-time in the verses and more motion in the choruses. The arc moves from solitary snowbound introspection through gathering urgency, warm resolute devotion, fragile bridge reflection, to a long tender final peak and a still quiet outro. The mix is wide and polished: bright plucked guzheng and breathy dizi above a warm rounded low end, spacious verses, dense gently compressed choruses, a bare bridge, an expansive final chorus, with tasteful reverbs and subtle reverse-swell transitions.\n\nVocal Details: A single female lead with a clear, crystalline, slightly breathy tone, warm in the low register and glowing on top, vibrato widening on long notes. Delivery is soft and narrative in the verses, builds through the pre-chorus, soars in the chorus with sustained belt, near-whisper in the bridge, and a long held note in the final chorus. Layered self-harmonies in parallel thirds and octaves widen the choruses, and a distant echoing backing voice floats in the outro; effects stay restrained, plate reverb, a subtle dotted delay on phrase tails, light compression.\n\nArrangement: Built around guzheng, dizi, piano and strings in one continuous arch: intro is solo guzheng over a low piano pedal with wind chimes and a faint pad, no percussion; verse one keeps the guzheng as the harmonic and melodic skeleton with warm piano, subtle bass, and a distant dizi line rising near the end; pre-chorus one builds over a swelling pad with reverse-cymbal swell while the groove settles on kick and finger-cymbals; the chorus arrives in full band - kick-and-snare, hi-hats, deep electric bass, string ensemble, guzheng glissandos, dizi ornaments and layered vocals; verse two recedes to piano arpeggios and muted guzheng over brush drum and string bed; the instrumental carries guzheng and dizi call-and-response over piano and strings with a long drum roll crescendo; the bridge strips to a single guzheng note and whisper-close voice in silence; the final chorus opens widest - upper-octave strings, stacked lines and a long held note - and the outro dissolves on the opening guzheng motif, a breath of dizi, one warm piano chord and fading wind chimes.",
				"[Intro]\n（古筝泛音 · 雪落声 · 一盏残灯）\n\n[Verse 1]\n雪满长安 一更又一更\n灯下研墨 写不完你的名\n一句心事 写到心思停\n落款处 只落一盏等\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\n月过寒窗 不肯分明\n隔着千山 谁替我传声\n\n[Chorus]\n借一轮月色 照你归程\n我点着一盏灯 守着孤城\n千里风雪 都是我的回声\n雪落一整夜 我醒着在等\n\n[Verse 2]\n石桥青苔 又落一层\n旧笛悬梁 冻到不肯醒\n夜深偏长 灯花结成影\n你名字落地 像雪压住长安的声\n\n[Pre-Chorus]\n呵出暖雾 窗上画你的名\n怕雪停以后 认不出这盏灯\n\n[Chorus]\n借一城的月光 引你归程\n我用一生 暖你这一盏灯\n风雪来回 就作我的歌声\n你回来 让雪落得安静\n\n[Instrumental]\n（古筝 · 竹笛 对答 · 雪落声）\n\n[Bridge]\n那年大雪 封冷了寒冬\n我们在两扇窗前 各守一盏灯\n若来世再有一盏 等你的灯\n我定比雪 更早亮到三更\n\n[Final Chorus]\n借一生的月色 唤你归程\n你门前的长明 便是我这一盏灯\n纵使大雪漫过 千山万城\n长安的灯海 都写着你的名\n\n[Outro]\n雪停了\n灯还亮着\n我还在城中\n等你回程",
				222,
				"fixed",
				300,
				1.5,
				50
			],
